<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <%#
      Site identity + trust/unfurl metadata. App-level (not the studio-engine
      head partial, which renders below) so we don't have to bump the gem.
      The engine head already ships viewport, csrf/csp, importmap, --nav-h,
      overflow-anchor, and apple-mobile-web-app-capable — none of which we
      touch here. og:url/og:image are absolute (wallet scanners + link
      unfurlers require absolute URLs). Title / description / share image
      resolve through OgHelper: a page-level content_for(:title) /
      content_for(:meta_description) / content_for(:og_image) override wins,
      then the admin-set SiteSetting defaults, then the hardcoded fallbacks.
    %>
    <%# Title/description/og:image resolve through OgHelper: a page-level
        content_for override wins, then the admin-set SiteSetting defaults
        (/admin/dashboard), then the hardcoded fallbacks below. See OgHelper. %>
    <% page_title = og_title(content_for(:title)) %>
    <% meta_description = og_description(content_for(:meta_description)) %>
    <%# og:image — a page-level content_for(:og_image) override wins; otherwise
        the admin-uploaded SiteSetting default, falling back to the static
        public/og.png (1200x630 landscape, ~53KB — sized + compressed for the
        unfurl; the old 1.3MB /logo.png timed out and blanked previews). Only
        the static fallback has known fixed dimensions. %>
    <% og_override = content_for(:og_image).presence %>
    <% resolved_og_image = og_override || og_image_url %>
    <% og_image_is_default = og_override.blank? && og_image_default? %>
    <title><%= page_title %></title>
    <meta name="description" content="<%= meta_description %>">

    <%# Favicons + PWA icons (Turf Monster green monster mark) %>
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/favicon.png">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="192x192" href="/icon-192.png">
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="512x512" href="/icon-512.png">
    <link rel="manifest" href="/site.webmanifest">

    <%# Brand dark navy (matches --color-page / manifest theme_color). %>
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#1e1b35">
    <%# Modern companion to the engine head's apple-mobile-web-app-capable
        (clears the Chrome deprecation warning). Both are intentionally kept. %>
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">

    <%# Open Graph + Twitter unfurl tags (shared with the landing layout). %>
    <%= render "layouts/link_preview_meta", title: page_title, description: meta_description,
          image: resolved_og_image, image_default: og_image_is_default %>

    <%# SRI-pinned (audit H2): exact versions + integrity so a poisoned CDN/package
        can't swap the signing-path crypto. web3.js @1 currently resolves to 1.98.4;
        pinning freezes that (re-pin + recompute the sha384 on any intentional bump).

        THE TWEETNACL TAG IS LOAD-BEARING, and BLOCKING is the load-bearing part.
        Both halves of the Phantom mobile round trip need window.nacl, and they
        come from DIFFERENT gems: the deep link from solana-studio
        (solana_studio/_phantom_deeplink, moved off studio-engine by
        /tasks/turf-rides-gem-modals) and the callback view from studio-engine
        (solana_sessions/phantom_callback, which never moved).
        The deep link reads it on a user tap (safe either way) but the callback
        view's IIFE reads it AT PARSE TIME and hard-fails with no retry. A
        blocking tag here means nacl is already defined when that runs.

        So this app deliberately renders NO deeplink_assets loader. solana-studio
        ships the only one (solana_studio/_deeplink_assets) and it APPENDS a
        script element, which is asynchronous, so the callback would race it.
        studio-engine shipped a second copy until /tasks/drop-engine-web3-modals
        dropped it in 0.66.2. That is ABOVE this app's 0.64.0 engine floor, not
        below it, so a resolver sitting at the floor still sees the engine's copy
        — which is why phantom_deeplink_adoption_test bans BOTH paths rather
        than just solana-studio's. Same integrity value, same
        URL, different timing. Swap to it only together with a callback that
        waits for nacl instead of failing. %>
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/@solana/web3.js@1.98.4/lib/index.iife.min.js"
            integrity="sha384-I45YF+S0YGWIolUyTksLk9TNtTqaDgZg8e6T1OoBoJvvFmphqYNIPZw3Kl0TkZNN"
            crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tweetnacl@1.0.3/nacl-fast.min.js"
            integrity="sha384-05+sicyRJQ56XpL4U9HJ8YbtSzFDvAg7apPKOGV6A0JsAJKFM68jp5oLnUjG5mEp"
            crossorigin="anonymous"></script>

    <%# THE REDIRECT TRANSPORT — the second way to reach a wallet.
        `walletProvider` models only an INJECTED provider, which no ordinary
        mobile browser has: on iOS Safari and Android Chrome detect() returns
        null, and before /tasks/guard-wallet-detect-mobile that null reached
        `.connect()` and printed a JavaScript error into a transaction modal.
        These four files are the other transport — the one that hands off to the
        wallet app by URL and resumes when it returns.

        ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is a dependency chain, not a preference:
        nacl above is read by the codec in wallet_transport; redirect_provider
        and wallet_ops both read SolanaStudio.walletTransport; wallet_ops also
        reaches for walletJournal. Each reads the one before it AT CALL TIME, so
        a wrong order fails on the first tap rather than at load — the worst
        place to find it.

        NACL STAYS BLOCKING AND ABOVE THEM for the reason its own tag records:
        the deep link reads it on a user tap (safe either way) but the callback
        view's IIFE reads it AT PARSE TIME and hard-fails with no retry.

        Served through sprockets from solana-studio, which declares all four in
        config.assets.precompile (lib/solana_studio/engine.rb) — a gem-shipped
        asset not named there 404s in production with no local warning. %>
    <%= javascript_include_tag "solana_studio/wallet_transport" %>
    <%= javascript_include_tag "solana_studio/redirect_provider" %>
    <%= javascript_include_tag "solana_studio/wallet_journal" %>
    <%= javascript_include_tag "solana_studio/wallet_ops" %>

    <%# Registered on EVERY page, because the wallet returns to a page this app
        does not own — see the partial for the entry that was lost when this
        lived in the contest board. %>
    <%# The HOW half of every on-chain flow — the return address, the cluster,
        the 401-normalising fetch, and the handoff-never-happened watch. FIRST,
        because the intents below use its fetch helper. It defines functions and
        runs nothing at load. %>
    <%= render "shared/wallet_op_runner" %>
    <%= render "shared/contest_entry_intent" %>
    <%# The other two intents, here for the same reason and rendered the same
        way. All three are idempotent and all three no-op on a page without
        wallet_ops.js. %>
    <%= render "shared/contest_create_intent" %>
    <%= render "shared/username_rename_intent" %>
    <% if session_replay_active? %>
      <script src="https://cdn.logr-in.com/LogRocket.min.js" crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
      <script>
        // LogRocket records DOM + network in prod. Sanitize before anything leaves the
        // browser (audit LR-1): never ship the CSRF token, an api-keyed RPC URL, or
        // auth/account/wallet request+response bodies (SIWS sigs, emails, tokens) to the
        // third-party recorder.
        //
        // The api-key rule is now DEFENCE IN DEPTH rather than the primary guard: since
        // redact-helius-key-from-browser the client is handed a credential-free endpoint
        // (Solana::Config.public_rpc_url), so no keyed URL should reach a client request
        // at all. Keep the rule — it costs nothing and covers any future rail that
        // reintroduces one. Defensive (try/catch) so a field-shape change can
        // only under-record, never throw or break init.
        window.LogRocket && window.LogRocket.init('jodsqq/mcritchie-studio', {
          dom: { inputSanitizer: true },
          network: {
            requestSanitizer: function (request) {
              try {
                var u = request.url || '';
                if (u.indexOf('api-key=') !== -1) request.url = u.replace(/api-key=[^&]+/gi, 'api-key=REDACTED');
                if (/\/(auth|account|wallet|magic_link|webhooks)(\/|\?|$)/.test(u)) request.body = undefined;
                if (request.headers) { delete request.headers['X-CSRF-Token']; delete request.headers['x-csrf-token']; }
              } catch (e) {}
              return request;
            },
            responseSanitizer: function (response) {
              try {
                var u = (response && response.url) || '';
                if (/\/(auth|account|wallet|magic_link|session_state)(\/|\?|$)/.test(u)) response.body = undefined;
              } catch (e) {}
              return response;
            }
          }
        });
      </script>
    <% end %>
    <% content_for :head do %>
      <script>
        <% if session_replay_active? && logged_in? && current_user %>
          <%# Identify by opaque slug only — never send email/PII to the replay
              vendor (Lazarus audit #2). Display name is already public on
              leaderboards; email is not. %>
          window.LogRocket && window.LogRocket.identify('<%= current_user.slug %>', {
            name: '<%= j(current_user.username || current_user.name || "") %>'
          });
        <% end %>
        <% if logged_in? && current_user.solana_connected? %>
          // Navbar hydrate. The server NO LONGER blocks the HTML render on the
          // USDC/USDT/seeds RPCs (perf: ~0.3-1s/page) — display_balance /
          // display_seeds_data render cache-first, nil ("loading") when cold.
          // So this fires refreshSession() RIGHT AWAY on every load (initial +
          // every Turbo nav). It is THE one page-load hydrate: pill + token
          // badge + seeds bar + $store.session cents + any data-wallet-tile
          // spans on the page (/account's balance tiles) all converge from
          // this single call. The nav spinner spins until it lands. A write
          // success path (entry confirm / token consume) still calls
          // refreshSession() directly for its own refresh.
          (function() {
            var _navHydrating = false;
            function hydrateNavbar() {
              if (_navHydrating) return;             // de-dupe initial + turbo:load
              // A SPEND JUST HAPPENED ON THE PAGE THAT SENT US HERE.
              //
              // This load-time hydrate is the read that painted the stale
              // number: contest creation navigates, so the marker its caller
              // left is consumed HERE (a setTimeout could not have survived the
              // unload). Reading now would very likely return the pre-spend
              // balance, so do not read and do not paint — hold the loading
              // state and let onchainSettled paint once, when it has settled.
              // ONE line of glue on purpose: the decision itself is
              // settleOnLoadIfPending() in solana_utils.js, where a unit test
              // can execute it. Inline layout script is unreachable by tests.
              // Claim the re-entry guard for the WHOLE settle window. This
              // handler fires on DOMContentLoaded AND turbo:load; without the
              // claim the second call skips the (now consumed) marker and does
              // the early read, undoing the defer a millisecond later.
              if (window.settleOnLoadIfPending &&
                  window.settleOnLoadIfPending(function () { _navHydrating = false; })) {
                _navHydrating = true;
                return;
              }
              _navHydrating = true;
              if (window.showNavSpinner) window.showNavSpinner();
              refreshSession().finally(function() {
                if (window.hideNavSpinner) window.hideNavSpinner();
                _navHydrating = false;
              });
            }
            document.addEventListener('turbo:load', hydrateNavbar);
            // Fire on initial load too — turbo:load may already have run (or
            // be racing) before this inline script registers its listener.
            if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
              document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', hydrateNavbar);
            } else {
              hydrateNavbar();
            }
          })();
        <% end %>

        // Invite link: capture ?ref= param into localStorage
        (function() {
          var params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
          var ref = params.get('ref');
          if (ref) {
            localStorage.setItem('inviter_slug', ref);
            params.delete('ref');
            var clean = window.location.pathname + (params.toString() ? '?' + params.toString() : '');
            history.replaceState(null, '', clean);
          }
        })();

        // Invite link: save inviter on login. Reads loggedIn from the
        // canonical #session-context JSON block — same source the Alpine
        // session store hydrates from. Doing it this way (rather than via
        // $store.session) sidesteps a listener-ordering nuance: this
        // turbo:load handler is registered BEFORE the session-store
        // re-hydration handler below, so at this point in the tick the
        // store may still hold the previous page's state. The JSON block
        // is freshly server-rendered on every nav, so it's always current.
        document.addEventListener('turbo:load', function() {
          var loggedIn = false;
          var ctx = document.getElementById('session-context');
          if (ctx) {
            try { loggedIn = !!JSON.parse(ctx.textContent).loggedIn; } catch (e) {}
          }
          var inviterSlug = localStorage.getItem('inviter_slug');
          if (loggedIn && inviterSlug) {
            fetch('/account/set_inviter', {
              method: 'PATCH',
              headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-CSRF-Token': document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content || '' },
              body: JSON.stringify({ inviter_slug: inviterSlug })
            }).then(function(r) {
              if (r.ok) localStorage.removeItem('inviter_slug');
            }).catch(function() {});
          }
        });
      </script>
      <script type="application/json" id="session-context"><%= client_session_payload.to_json.html_safe %></script>
      <script>
        // --- Inline Solana stores + components ---
        // Alpine's defer script fires alpine:init BEFORE importmap modules execute.
        // These must be inline so they're available when Alpine processes the DOM.
        // The full wallet_provider.js module overwrites this stub after modules load.

        // Mark a freshly-created user so the profile modal auto-opens after
        // the post-verify page reload. Called from every site that
        // successfully verifies a Solana sign-in: the inline connect()
        // below (initial connect), solana_stores.js _reauth() (silent
        // wallet-switch re-auth), and solana_sessions/phantom_callback
        // (Phantom mobile deep-link return). The localStorage round-trip
        // is intentional — it survives the window.location reload that
        // ends each of those flows; the simpler alternative (Rails flash
        // via SessionContext) would couple SessionContext to flash, which
        // it currently avoids by design.
        // Post-Solana-verify hook. The new-Phantom-signup avatar prompt (the
        // 'profile' modal) was retired — usernames auto-generate, nothing to
        // complete — so this is now a no-op extension point for future hooks.
        window.handleSolanaVerifySuccess = function(result) {};

        // Minimal wallet provider stub — enough for Alpine x-data evaluation
        window.walletProvider = {
          isAvailable: function() {
            return !!(window.__WALLET_KEYPAIR_SECRET) || !!(window.phantom && window.phantom.solana && window.phantom.solana.isPhantom);
          },
          isMobile: function() {
            return /Android|iPhone|iPad|iPod/i.test(navigator.userAgent) || (navigator.maxTouchPoints > 1 && /Macintosh/.test(navigator.userAgent));
          },
          detect: function() {
            return null;
          },
          // Empty until the full wallet_provider module loads + wallets
          // register via Wallet Standard. The hub reads this at click time.
          available: function() {
            return [];
          },
          // MIRRORED FROM THE MODULE, and it has to be. Alpine evaluates x-data
          // before importmap modules execute, so a click landing in that window
          // reaches THIS object — and a stub missing the method would throw
          // "requireProvider is not a function", which is the same class of
          // unreadable failure this pair of methods exists to remove. The stub's
          // detect() always returns null, so the honest message is the only
          // answer it can give, and it is the right one.
          requireProvider: function() {
            var provider = this.detect();
            if (provider) return provider;
            throw new Error(this.noWalletMessage());
          },
          // MIRRORED BECAUSE A PRE-HYDRATION CALLER ALREADY REACHES FOR IT.
          // shared/_alpine_factories asks for requireInlineProvider(), and this
          // stub is what answers during the window before importmap modules
          // execute — so a stub without it throws "requireInlineProvider is not
          // a function", the same unreadable failure the pair above exists to
          // remove. The stub's detect() always answers null, so the honest
          // message is the only answer it can give, and it is the right one.
          requireInlineProvider: function() {
            var provider = this.detect();
            if (provider && provider.transport !== 'redirect') return provider;
            throw new Error(this.noWalletMessage());
          },
          noWalletMessage: function() {
            if (this.isMobile()) {
              return "This browser cannot reach a wallet. Open this page inside your " +
                     "wallet app's own browser — Phantom, Solflare, and Backpack each " +
                     "have one — then try again.";
            }
            return "No wallet detected. Install or unlock a Solana wallet extension, " +
                   "then refresh this page.";
          },
          // MIRRORED FOR THE SAME REASON, and this pair needs it MORE than the
          // one above. The admin desktop-only notice PAINTS at parse time, from
          // an inline script in the page body — so the object answering it is
          // always THIS one, never the module. A stub without these two throws
          // "desktopOnlyMessage is not a function" while painting the very
          // element whose job is to keep a phone from hitting an error.
          // test/integration/wallet_stub_parity_test.rb runs both objects and
          // compares what they SAY, so a divergence here goes red.
          desktopOnlyMessage: function() {
            return "This operation is desktop only. Open the page on a desktop " +
                   "browser with your wallet extension — a phone cannot reach a " +
                   "signer wallet.";
          },
          requireDesktop: function() {
            if (this.isMobile()) throw new Error(this.desktopOnlyMessage());
          }
        };

        // Shared SIWS connect+verify, used by the wallet-connect modal (and
        // reusable anywhere). Picks a provider by name, signs the nonce, posts
        // to verify (or link_solana when linkMode), and RESOLVES the server
        // result — the caller decides where to navigate. Inline (not a module)
        // so it's available the moment the modal mounts.
        window.solanaConnectAndVerify = async function(walletName, opts) {
          opts = opts || {};
          var provider = walletName ? window.walletProvider.get(walletName) : window.walletProvider.detect();
          if (!provider) throw new Error('Wallet not available');
          // Fire the nonce fetch IMMEDIATELY. It takes no parameters and does not
          // depend on the wallet, so starting it here lets the round trip overlap
          // whatever comes next.
          //
          // TAGGED REJECTION. This is a fetch of OUR OWN server, and its failure
          // must never be read as a broken wallet. The tag is what makes the
          // fallback path's single catch able to tell the two apart, and that is
          // what buys back the overlap: the `await` can sit BELOW
          // provider.connect(), where the round trip runs against the human at
          // the wallet prompt, instead of being hoisted above the try purely so
          // the catch could not misread it. Correctness by evidence rather than
          // by keeping the statement out of reach.
          //
          // SIDE EFFECT, deliberate and recorded: the server's NONCE_MAX_AGE
          // (300s) clock starts here, before the wallet sheet opens, so those
          // five minutes cover human think-time at the prompt. It fails CLOSED —
          // a user who dwells past five minutes gets a clean "Nonce expired" and
          // retries — which is why the trade is worth a saved round trip.
          //
          // OUR SERVER'S OWN SENTENCE, COMPOSED ONCE. Measured 2026-09-08 on the
          // code this replaces: a nonce fetch that 500s with an HTML body makes
          // r.json() reject with "Unexpected token '<', \"<!DOCTYPE \"... is not
          // valid JSON", parseSolanaError's /^unexpected/i branch reads that as a
          // TRANSACTION failure, and the user is told to check their wallet
          // connection and their USDC balance. An outage of ours, read back to a
          // paying user as their wallet being short of funds.
          //
          // SURVIVES THE MAPPER, which is the whole reason it can be substituted
          // rather than rethrown — parseSolanaError passes unrecognised messages
          // through untouched, and test/views/nonce_server_failure_copy_test.rb
          // runs this literal past every regex the mapper tests a message against.
          var nonceServerCopy = 'Our server could not start sign-in \u2014 the problem is on our side, not your wallet. Please try again in a moment.';
          var noncePromise = fetch('/auth/solana/nonce')
            .then(function(r) {
              // A BODY WE CANNOT READ IS OUR SERVER FAILING, AND THIS IS THE ONE
              // PLACE THAT KNOWS IT. Below, in the single .catch, an offline
              // TypeError and an unreadable 500 body are the same rejection and
              // cannot be told apart; here they are already separated by WHICH
              // step threw. So the substitution belongs here — inside r.json() —
              // and the network failure keeps its own words on the way past.
              //
              // BOTH WALLET PATHS, FROM ONE PLACE. The signIn branch awaits this
              // promise ABOVE its try (the nonce is an INPUT to signIn), so no
              // catch in this function ever sees its rejection — it propagates
              // straight to the surface. A substitution at the nonceFetchFailed
              // guard alone would therefore have left every signIn-capable wallet,
              // Phantom included, still reading the balance advice. Measured on
              // both paths, 2026-09-08.
              return r.json().catch(function() {
                var serverError = new Error(nonceServerCopy);
                // NOT REPORTED — the one substitution in this file that owes no
                // report, and it owes none for the reason docs/AUTH.md already
                // gives for a server REJECTION: report-client-wallet-failures
                // exists for failures that leave NO server-side trace, because
                // they happen entirely in the browser. This one is a 500 OF OURS;
                // the exception, its backtrace and the request are already in the
                // server's own log, and the POST would go to the very server that
                // just failed. The string being replaced is V8's complaint about
                // our error page, not a wallet's words, so nothing diagnostic
                // dies here.
                //
                // TAGGED ANYWAY, so no SURFACE reports it either. Without the tag
                // the wallet-setup modal's catch would file a row carrying our
                // sentence in BOTH halves — the raw == mapped shape docs/AUTH.md
                // names as an anomaly worth chasing — for a fault that is fully
                // recorded server-side.
                serverError.walletFailureReported = true;
                throw serverError;
              });
            })
            .catch(function(e) {
              // Tag the error the fetch actually produced rather than wrapping
              // it: a wrapper would drop `TypeError: Failed to fetch` — the one
              // string that says "offline" — and replace it with our own guess.
              // The substituted server sentence above arrives here as an object
              // too, so it picks up the same tag and the fallback's guard goes on
              // rethrowing a nonce failure untouched, whichever shape it took.
              if (e && typeof e === 'object') { e.nonceFetchFailed = true; throw e; }
              var wrapped = new Error(String(e));
              wrapped.nonceFetchFailed = true;
              throw wrapped;
            });
          // Now that the fallback awaits this BELOW connect(), a connect() that
          // rejects first leaves the rejection unawaited — a console
          // "Uncaught (in promise)" on a path that already reported its real
          // error. Attaching a no-op handler marks it handled; the promise
          // itself is untouched, so the await below still rejects normally.
          noncePromise.catch(function() {});

          var domain = window.location.host;
          var userIdLine = (opts.linkMode && opts.currentUserId) ? ('User-ID: ' + opts.currentUserId + '\n\n') : '';
          var pubkeyB58, message, signatureB58;

          // ONE approval when the wallet implements solana:signIn — it connects and
          // signs in a single prompt. Everything else keeps the two-step path.
          var useSignIn = typeof provider.supportsSignIn === 'function' && provider.supportsSignIn();

          if (useSignIn) {
            // AWAITED ABOVE THE TRY HERE, AND THAT IS NOT THE FALLBACK'S BUG.
            // signIn() takes the nonce as an INPUT, so there is no prompt for the
            // fetch to overlap on this path — it must land first either way. Out
            // here it also stays out of the catch below, which decides whether
            // the wallet is INCAPABLE of signIn and falls back: a server blip
            // swallowed there would demote a perfectly good signIn wallet to the
            // two-prompt path. The fallback awaits BELOW its connect() because
            // there the fetch has a human-gated prompt to run against, and its
            // catch tells a nonce failure apart by tag rather than by distance.
            var nonceData = await noncePromise;
            try {
              // SIWS `statement` is a SINGLE LINE per the spec's grammar, so the
              // link-mode binding rides inline here rather than as its own
              // paragraph the way the fallback message writes it. The server checks
              // `message.include?("User-ID: <id>")`, which both shapes satisfy.
              var statement = userIdLine
                ? 'Sign in to Turf Monster (User-ID: ' + opts.currentUserId + ')'
                : 'Sign in to Turf Monster';

              var out = await provider.signIn({ domain: domain, statement: statement, nonce: nonceData.nonce });
              pubkeyB58 = out.address;
              // The bytes the WALLET signed — never a rebuilt string. A wallet may
              // add URI / Version / Chain ID / Issued At lines of its own, and a
              // reconstruction would not match the signature.
              message = new TextDecoder().decode(out.signedMessage);
              signatureB58 = encodeBase58(out.signature);

              // Did the wallet honour what we asked for? A message missing our nonce
              // — or, in link mode, the User-ID binding OPSEC-005 requires — gets
              // rejected server-side as a bad signature, which reads to the user as
              // "sign-in is broken" rather than "this wallet composes messages its
              // own way". Catch it here and spend a second prompt instead of failing.
              // The DOMAIN is the third thing the server asserts and the one most
              // easily lost: AuthVerifier requires the message to OPEN with
              // `request.host_with_port + " "` (OPSEC-018). A wallet that rewrites
              // or port-strips the domain token produces a hard 401 with no
              // fallback — and spends the nonce on the way. Production is safe (no
              // port in the host); a dev/QA stack on localhost:31xx is where it
              // bites, which is exactly where this gets exercised.
              if (message.indexOf(domain + ' ') !== 0) {
                throw new Error('wallet rewrote the domain');
              }
              if (message.indexOf('Nonce: ' + nonceData.nonce) === -1) {
                throw new Error('wallet omitted the nonce');
              }
