          // this was a bare `return await (await fetch(...)).json()` sitting
          // outside BOTH try blocks. A reply that carries a rendered PAGE makes
          // `.json()` reject with V8's
          // "Unexpected token '<', \"<!DOCTYPE \"... is not valid JSON";
          // parseSolanaError's /^unexpected/i branch reads that as a TRANSACTION
          // failure and answers "check wallet connection and USDC balance".
          //
          // WHICH FAULTS ACTUALLY SEND AN UNREADABLE BODY — NOT "any unhandled
          // exception". That was this comment's claim until 2026-09-09 and it is
          // FALSE on both endpoints: SolanaSessionsController#verify and
          // AccountsController#link_solana each END in a generic
          // `rescue StandardError` that renders JSON 422, which parses fine. An
          // exception raised INSIDE either action is therefore not this case at
          // all. THREE things do send HTML, and TWO OF THEM ARRIVE AT STATUS 200:
          //
          //   * THE ENGINE CATCH-ALL. Studio::ErrorHandling registers
          //     `rescue_from StandardError`, so anything the actions' own rescues
          //     do not claim — a raise in one of the before_actions this POST
          //     runs, or in /auth/solana/nonce, which has no local rescue —
          //     reaches handle_unexpected_error. In production that answers
          //     through `respond_to`, and this fetch sends no Accept header, so
          //     it takes `format.html`: a 302 to root, which fetch FOLLOWS to an
          //     HTML body at status 200;
          //   * THE OPSEC-045 FORCED LOGOUT. A session_token mismatch in
          //     ApplicationController#verify_session_token answers the same way —
          //     a 302 to /signin, followed to an HTML body at status 200.
          //     Measured end to end 2026-09-09;
          //   * A FAULT OUTSIDE rescue_from's REACH — middleware, routing — which
          //     ActionDispatch::PublicExceptions renders as the 500 error page.
          //
          // `r.ok` is TRUE on the first two. That is the whole argument for why
          // the substitution below sits inside `.json()` and can never become an
          // `!r.ok` gate — such a gate would not fire at all on the two shapes
          // this app actually produces most.
          //
          // WORSE THAN THE NONCE CASE BECAUSE OF WHEN IT FIRES. The nonce fetch
          // happens BEFORE the user signs. This one happens AFTER the signature
          // succeeded — so on a real-money product a user signs, our server
          // faults, and we tell them they are short of funds at the exact moment
          // they have most reason to think something was taken. The one step that
          // proved their wallet fine is the step that precedes the accusation.
          //
          // ONE SITE, BOTH PATHS — and unlike the nonce leg, that is free here.
          // The signIn branch and the connect + signMessage fallback both fall
          // through to this line; neither wraps it. So a substitution here covers
          // every wallet, and no guard-local variant is needed.
          var verifyServerCopy = 'Your signature went through, but our server could not finish sign-in \u2014 the problem is on our side, not your wallet. Signing in moves no funds. Please try again in a moment.';
          // NO `Accept: application/json` HERE, AND THAT IS THE DELIBERATE
          // CHOICE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Two sibling call sites in this file send
          // it (postMagicLink below, and the nonce fetch takes the default), so
          // adding it here reads as a tidy-up. It would DISARM the guard on the
          // next line. Measured 2026-09-09 against ActionDispatch::PublicExceptions:
          // with no Accept header — or `*/*`, or `text/html` — a 500 comes back
          // as the rendered error PAGE, which is the body `.json()` rejects on;
          // with `Accept: application/json` the same 500 comes back as
          // {"status":500,"error":"Internal Server Error"}, which PARSES. The
          // helper would then resolve that object, the substitution would never
          // run, and the user would read a bare "Internal Server Error" instead
          // of a sentence telling them their signature moved no funds — while
          // every assertion about the guard stayed green, because the guard is
          // simply never reached.
          //
          // So the absence of this header is load-bearing and is pinned as such:
          // test/views/verify_server_failure_copy_test.rb drives the RENDERED
          // helper against a server that answers by Accept the way Rails does,
          // and reddens the moment this header appears. If a future change wants
          // the header, it has to re-aim the guard in the same diff — a JSON
          // error body needs a STATUS test, which the note below explains this
          // endpoint cannot have naively (401/422 carry bodies the user must read).
          var verifyResponse = await fetch(verifyUrl, {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-CSRF-Token': document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content || '' },
            body: JSON.stringify({ message: message, signature: signatureB58, pubkey: pubkeyB58, age_attestation: ageAttestation, wallet_provider: walletProviderName })
          });
          // SUBSTITUTED INSIDE r.json(), FOR THE SAME REASON THE NONCE LEG IS,
          // AND DELIBERATELY NOT AT `!r.ok`. docs/AUTH.md used to note that
          // "nothing checks r.ok" as though the check were the fix. It is not:
          // this endpoint answers 401 and 422 with a JSON body the user MUST
          // read — an expired nonce, a bad signature, the age attestation
          // (SolanaSessionsController#verify renders all three) — and the modal
          // paints `result.error` from it. A throw on !r.ok would replace those
          // precise, actionable sentences with this generic one, which is the
          // same class of confidently-wrong diagnosis pointed at a new party.
          // Only an UNREADABLE body is unambiguously our fault, and `.json()`
          // rejecting is exactly the signal that says so.
          //
          // AND THE OFFLINE USER STAYS OUT OF IT. Wrapping the fetch itself —
          // the obvious simplification — makes `TypeError: Failed to fetch` and
          // an unreadable 500 body the same rejection, and relabels a genuinely
          // offline user as our outage. Here they are already separated by WHICH
          // step threw, so a request that never reached us keeps its own words
          // and stays reportable. Mutated both ways in
          // test/views/verify_server_failure_copy_test.rb.
          //
          // NOT REPORTED, tagged so no surface reports it either — the reasoning
          // is the nonce leg's, unchanged: this is a 500 OF OURS, already carrying
          // its exception and backtrace in the server's own log, and the report
          // POST would go to the very server that just failed. The string being
          // replaced is V8's complaint about our error page, not a wallet's
          // words, so nothing diagnostic dies here. Without the tag the
          // wallet-setup modal's catch files a row with our sentence in BOTH
          // halves — the raw == mapped shape docs/AUTH.md names as an anomaly.
          return await verifyResponse.json().catch(function() {
            var serverError = new Error(verifyServerCopy);
            serverError.walletFailureReported = true;
            throw serverError;
          });
        };

        // Shared magic-link request. Form-encoded (so the rack-attack per-email
        // throttle, which reads req.params before JSON parsing, applies) with
        // Accept: json for the response. opts.contest / opts.picks ride along
        // for the in-contest flow so the emailed link returns to the contest.
        // Resolves the parsed JSON. Inline so every scope (standalone form,
        // board, modal) shares one request shape.
        window.postMagicLink = async function(email, opts) {
